.0222320556640625, -0.02435302734375, 0.00682830810546875, -0.02740478515625, -0.00971221923828125, 0.023223876953125, 0.026580810546875, 0.043548583984375, 0.0084381103515625, 0.00580596923828125, 0.0307769775390625, 0.0096435546875, -0.050140380859375, 0.0059356689453125, -0.00696563720703125, 0.017364501953125, -0.0037212371826171875, -0.01277923583984375, 0.005001068115234375, -0.02642822265625, -0.033172607421875, 0.0144195556640625, -0.01165008544921875, 0.0001919269561767578, 0.0004248619079589844, -0.0173492431640625, -0.01885986328125, 0.0080108642578125, -0.01390838623046875, -0.0189971923828125, 0.0248565673828125, -0.005161285400390625, 0.0218505859375, 0.01220703125, -0.034942626953125, -0.00359344482421875, -0.0011568069458007812, 0.01325225830078125, -0.001590728759765625, -0.005153656005859375, 0.0113372802734375, -0.009033203125, 0.02294921875, -0.01287841796875, -0.00902557373046875, 0.03472900390625, -0.01416015625, 0.01473236083984375, 0.0204010009765625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent research has explored the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in electrical engineering. GA provides a unified mathematical language for representing geometric transformations and relationships, often simplifying the analysis of multidimensional systems and offering new insights into their structure compared to traditional vector or matrix approaches.\n\n**Definitions and Notation.**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\) denote the real geometric (Clifford) algebra of signature \\((p,q)\\), generated by an orthonormal basis \\(\\{e_1, \\ldots, e_{p+q}\\}\\) with \\(e_i^2 = 1\\) for \\(1 \\leq i \\leq p\\) and \\(e_i^2 = -1\\) for \\(p < i \\leq p+q\\). A *multivector* in \\(\\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\) is a linear combination of basis blades (elements of different grades). A *multivector-valued function* is a function \\(f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\), i.e., for each \\(t \\in \\mathbb{R}\\), \\(f(t)\\) is a multivector. The *exponential map* for a multivector \\(A\\) is defined by the power series \\(e^{A} = \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty \\frac{A^k}{k!}\\), but closed forms are generally available only for special cases (e.g., blades or bivectors).\n\n**Review of Prior Work.**  \nExtensions of the Laplace transform to hypercomplex algebras have been considered in the literature. Hitzer & Sangwine (2013) define a quaternionic Laplace transform, but their approach is limited to quaternions and does not generalize to arbitrary Clifford or geometric algebras. Brackx et al. (2001) and De Bie et al. (2012) develop Laplace and Fourier transforms in Clifford analysis, but typically restrict the Laplace parameter to scalars and focus on monogenic (Clifford-analytic) functions, avoiding the complications of non-commutative exponentials. Ablamowicz & Fauser (2004) discuss the exponential map in Clifford algebras, highlighting the lack of closed forms for general multivectors and the computational complexity in higher dimensions. None of these works provide an explicit, general definition of the Laplace transform for arbitrary multivector-valued functions in low-dimensional geometric algebras, nor do they systematically address the role of the Laplace parameter or the exponential map in this context.\n\n**Definition of the Laplace Transform in GA.**  \nOur main contribution is a rigorous and explicit definition of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions in \\(\\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\), for signatures with \\(p+q \\leq 5\\). For a function \\(f: [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\), we define\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}[f](s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\, dt,\n\\]\nwhere \\(s \\in \\mathbb{R}\\) is a real scalar. Here, \\(e^{-st}\\) is the standard scalar exponential, which commutes with all multivectors in the integrand. This ensures that the integral is well-defined and that the Laplace transform retains linearity and other classical properties.\n\n**Justification for Scalar Restriction.**  \nAllowing \\(s\\) to be a general multivector leads to significant mathematical obstacles. In general, for \\(A, B \\in \\mathcal{G}(p,q)\\), \\(e^{A} B \\neq B e^{A}\\) unless \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) commute. If \\(s\\) is not scalar, the ordering of \\(e^{-st}\\) and \\(f(t)\\) in the integrand affects the result, and the exponential \\(e^{-st}\\) may not be well-defined for arbitrary \\(s\\). For example, if \\(s\\) is a bivector, \\(e^{-st}\\) involves trigonometric or hyperbolic functions of multivectors, and the product \\(e^{-st} f(t)\\) is not generally associative or commutative. This non-commutativity complicates the analysis and can lead to ambiguities or ill-defined integrals, as discussed in Ablamowicz & Fauser (2004). Previous attempts to generalize the Laplace transform to non-scalar parameters (see Brackx et al., 2001) have either imposed severe restrictions on the class of admissible functions or avoided the issue entirely by restricting \\(s\\) to scalars. Thus, we restrict \\(s\\) to real scalars to ensure mathematical consistency and to preserve the essential properties of the Laplace transform.\n\n**Scope and Dimensionality.**  \nOur results are explicitly developed for geometric algebras with \\(p+q \\leq 5\\). This restriction is crucial for two reasons: (1) In higher dimensions, the number of basis elements grows exponentially (\\(2^{p+q}\\)), making explicit computation of the exponential map and the Laplace transform intractable; (2) Closed-form expressions for the exponential of a general multivector are unavailable beyond low dimensions, as shown in Ablamowicz & Fauser (2004). All explicit calculations and examples in this work rely on this low-dimensional setting. In principle, some theoretical properties (e.g., linearity, existence for sufficiently decaying functions) may extend to higher dimensions, but practical computation and explicit inversion formulas become infeasible.\n\n**Worked Example: RLC Circuit in \\(\\mathcal{G}(3,0)\\).**  \nConsider a series RLC circuit where the current \\(I(t)\\) and voltage \\(V(t)\\) are represented as multivector-valued functions in \\(\\mathcal{G}(3,0)\\). Let\n\\[\nI(t) = I_1(t) e_1 + I_2(t) e_2 + I_3(t) e_3,\n\\]\nwhere \\(I_k(t)\\) are real-valued functions representing current components along three orthogonal directions. The circuit equation is\n\\[\nL \\frac{dI}{dt} + R I(t) + \\frac{1}{C} \\int_0^t I(\\tau) d\\tau = V(t),\n\\]\nwhere \\(L, R, C\\) are real scalars. Applying the Laplace transform (componentwise, since the basis vectors commute with scalars), we obtain\n\\[\nL s \\mathcal{L}[I](s) + R \\mathcal{L}[I](s) + \\frac{1}{C s} \\mathcal{L}[I](s) = \\mathcal{L}[V](s).\n\\]\nThus,\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}[I](s) = \\left( L s + R + \\frac{1}{C s} \\right)^{-1} \\mathcal{L}[V](s),\n\\]\nwhere the inverse is taken in the algebra. Since all coefficients are scalars, the inversion is straightforward, and the solution preserves the multivector structure of \\(I(t)\\), allowing geometric information (e.g., current orientation) to be tracked throughout the analysis. For example, if \\(V(t) = V_0 e_1 \\sin(\\omega t)\\), then \\(\\mathcal{L}[V](s) = V_0 e_1 \\frac{\\omega}{s^2 + \\omega^2}\\), and the solution for \\(\\mathcal{L}[I](s)\\) is a multivector-valued rational function, which can be inverted (via partial fractions) to recover \\(I(t)\\) in the time domain.\n\n**Novelty and Comparison with Prior Work.**  \nThe novelty of our approach lies in: (1) providing an explicit, general definition of the Laplace transform for arbitrary multivector-valued functions in low-dimensional geometric algebras; (2) clarifying the necessity and implications of restricting the Laplace parameter to scalars, with rigorous justification; and (3) demonstrating, through explicit calculation, how geometric information is preserved in practical applications such as circuit analysis. Previous works have either focused on special cases (e.g., quaternions, monogenic functions) or avoided the technical challenges of non-commutative exponentials by imposing restrictive assumptions. Our approach systematically addresses these issues and delineates the precise scope of applicability.\n\n**Limitations and Open Problems.**  \nSeveral limitations remain. First, the restriction to scalar \\(s\\) precludes the use of more general Laplace parameters, which might be desirable for certain classes of GA-valued differential equations. The invertibility of expressions like \\(s + a\\), where \\(a\\) is a multivector, is not guaranteed in general and may fail for certain choices of \\(a\\). The behavior of the Laplace transform under GA operations (e.g., products, reverses, grade projections) warrants further investigation, especially in the context of non-commutative or non-associative products. Extending the approach to higher-dimensional algebras is theoretically possible for some properties, but explicit computation and inversion become impractical. Open problems include: (1) developing efficient algorithms for the exponential map in higher dimensions; (2) characterizing the class of admissible functions for which the Laplace transform exists and is invertible in GA; and (3) exploring the possibility of meaningful Laplace transforms with non-scalar parameters, perhaps for restricted"}}